# Transforming Teaching with AI

Review the story below about a teacher planning, preparing, and reflecting on instruction.

👉 Click on the highlighted quotes to explore how AI could support her work at different points in the process.

---

<div style="float:right; width:260px; margin:0 0 12px 20px;">
  <img 
    src="https://images.code.org/fe7e8f06adafd4a2681cd08a5e718cc2-b9e3f4df-dd95-461e-95dc-dac0d18d9c43.png"
    alt="Cartoon ecosystem illustration"
    style="width:100%; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid #D9DDE3;"
  >
</div>

Ms. Garcia sits down after school to plan tomorrow’s 7th-grade science lesson on ecosystem relationships. She has a clear learning goal and knows the key concepts students need to understand, but she wants the lesson to feel engaging and relevant rather than another textbook-based discussion.

As she begins planning, she reviews last year’s materials and searches online for activity ideas. She finds several possibilities, but none quite fit the lesson she has in mind. After spending several minutes searching, she catches herself thinking:

<details style="display:inline;"> <summary style="
cursor:pointer;
display:inline;
font-size:15px;
font-weight:400;
line-height:1.5;
color:#555;
"> <div style="
margin:8px 0 8px 24px;
padding-left:12px;
border-left:3px solid #DDD6F3;
color:#666;
"> <span style="font-size:0.7em;">▶ </span><i>"I have a solid explanation of food webs, but I can't think of a hands-on activity that would help students actively explore the concept."</i>
</div> </summary>
  
<div style="
margin-top:10px;
margin-left:20px;
margin-bottom:20px;
background:#F7F8FA;
border:1px solid #D9DDE3;
border-radius:10px;
padding:12px 14px;
font-size:15px;
line-height:1.5;
color:#555;
">🤖 AI can help brainstorm lesson activities, lesson hooks, and real-world examples.</div> </details>

With time running short and no better idea emerging, she decides to reuse the worksheet from last year and moves on to the next part of the lesson.

Ms. Garcia reviews the state-assigned reading passage students will use during class. As she reads through it, she realizes that several students in this year's class read well below grade level and will likely struggle with the text. She spends time rewriting sentences, simplifying vocabulary, and shortening sections to make the passage more accessible.

When she finally finishes, she's still unsure whether she struck the right balance between accessibility and rigor.

<details style="display:inline;"> <summary style="
cursor:pointer;
display:inline;
font-size:15px;
